# 17. Boundary Traversal

**a2z-315**

Order:

```text
Root
→ Left Boundary
→ Leaf Nodes
→ Right Boundary reversed
```

## Java

```java
public static List<Integer> boundary(TreeNode root) {

    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

    if (root == null)
        return result;

    // Root is added once.
    if (!isLeaf(root))
        result.add(root.val);

    addLeftBoundary(root.left, result);
    addLeaves(root, result);
    addRightBoundary(root.right, result);

    return result;
}

private static void addLeftBoundary(
        TreeNode root,
        List<Integer> result) {

    while (root != null) {

        if (!isLeaf(root))
            result.add(root.val);

        // Prefer left child.
        root = root.left != null
             ? root.left
             : root.right;
    }
}

private static void addRightBoundary(
        TreeNode root,
        List<Integer> result) {

    List<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();

    while (root != null) {

        if (!isLeaf(root))
            temp.add(root.val);

        root = root.right != null
             ? root.right
             : root.left;
    }

    // Reverse because right boundary is bottom-up.
    for (int i = temp.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        result.add(temp.get(i));
}

private static void addLeaves(
        TreeNode root,
        List<Integer> result) {

    if (root == null)
        return;

    if (isLeaf(root)) {
        result.add(root.val);
        return;
    }

    addLeaves(root.left, result);
    addLeaves(root.right, result);
}

private static boolean isLeaf(TreeNode root) {
    return root.left == null &&
           root.right == null;
}
```

