# 14. Maximum Path Sum

**a2z-312**

A path can start/end at any nodes.

At each node:

```text
left contribution
right contribution
```

But when returning to parent, we can only take **one side**.

## Java

```java
public static int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
    int[] answer = {Integer.MIN_VALUE};

    maxGain(root, answer);

    return answer[0];
}

private static int maxGain(
        TreeNode root,
        int[] answer) {

    if (root == null)
        return 0;

    // Negative contribution can only hurt the path.
    int left = Math.max(
        0,
        maxGain(root.left, answer)
    );

    int right = Math.max(
        0,
        maxGain(root.right, answer)
    );

    // Path passing through current node.
    int pathThroughNode =
        root.val + left + right;

    answer[0] = Math.max(
        answer[0],
        pathThroughNode
    );

    // Parent can continue through only one branch.
    return root.val + Math.max(left, right);
}
```

## C#

```csharp
public static int MaxPathSum(TreeNode root)
{
    int answer = int.MinValue;

    MaxGain(root, ref answer);

    return answer;
}

private static int MaxGain(
    TreeNode root,
    ref int answer)
{
    if (root == null)
        return 0;

    // Ignore negative branches.
    int left = Math.Max(
        0,
        MaxGain(root.Left, ref answer)
    );

    int right = Math.Max(
        0,
        MaxGain(root.Right, ref answer)
    );

    // Path can use both branches at this node.
    int path =
        root.Val + left + right;

    answer = Math.Max(answer, path);

    // Parent can use only one branch.
    return root.Val + Math.Max(left, right);
}
```

**Time:** `O(N)`
**Space:** `O(H)`

